**Cold-call script**

The first call’s objective is to get a short walkthrough with the decision-maker. Use the preview to make the conversation specific.

**When someone answers**

“Hi, this is Vincent with Phan-Rodriguez. Who would be the best person to speak with about a website for the business?”

**Once you reach that person**

“Hi [name], I’m Vincent. My partner and I build websites for businesses around the Inland Empire. I put together a sample website for [business], and I’m calling to see whether it would be useful to you. Have you got 30 seconds?”

**For Tri Auto**

“When I looked up Tri Auto, I found strong reviews, but I wasn’t sure which website was yours. Do you already have one that you use?”

[Listen. If they have one, ask for the address before continuing.]

“The sample I made puts your Toyota, Honda, and Lexus work up front, then makes it easy for someone on their phone to see your services, call, and get directions. I’d want to confirm those details with you.”

“Are you looking to bring in more of a particular kind of repair work, or is the shop already as busy as you want?”

[Listen. Connect the demo to their actual answer.]

**Invite them to see it**

“Would you be open to a five-minute look? You can tell me whether it fits the shop before we discuss doing anything with it.”

[If yes:]

“What’s the best email address to send the preview to? And would [specific time] or [specific time] work for a quick walkthrough?”

**If they ask the price immediately**

“For the one-page site I’m describing, it’s $1,500 to finish and launch, including two rounds of revisions. Ongoing hosting and small updates are optional at $49 a month. Your domain would be in your name and billed separately at cost.”

**After showing the preview**

“What would you change so this accurately represents the shop?”

[Listen and establish the scope.]

“If we make those changes, does this look like something you’d want to use?”

[If yes:]

“I can send a short proposal listing exactly what’s included. It’s $750 to start and $750 after you approve the finished site, before launch.”

