**Responses to common objections**

**“We already have Google/Yelp/Facebook.”**

“That makes sense. The website would give you a page you control, with your services and contact information together. Is there anything customers regularly have trouble finding or keep calling to ask?”

**“We get plenty of business.”**

“Understood. Is there a particular kind of work you’d prefer more of, or are you happy with the mix you’re getting?”

[If they’re satisfied and uninterested, thank them and end the call.]

**“That’s too expensive.”**

“Is it the total price, or paying it upfront?”

[If payment timing is the issue:]

“We could split the $1,500 into three $500 payments tied to starting, approving the design, and launching.”

[If the total is the issue:]

“We could reduce the scope. What would you need the website to do?”

**“Just send me something.”**

“Absolutely. What’s the best email address? Would it be okay if I follow up on [day] after you’ve had a chance to look?”

**“Will it get us more customers?”**

“That’s the goal, but I can’t promise a number. It gives people a clearer way to understand your services and contact you. We can also set up measurement for visits and call-button clicks, though a click doesn’t necessarily mean a completed call or a booked job.”

**“Not interested.”**

“Understood. Thanks for your time.”

Start with **Tri Auto at $1,500**, show the preview on a phone, and ask whether they actually want more work before pitching growth. If price becomes the sticking point, adjust scope or payment timing before discounting the same deliverable.
